      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please document the permission/validation model in the Put Content section of `rest-api-projects.txt` — the current prose reads like a push. Suggested wording:\n\n```\n\u003e This is a server-side branch update, not a `git push`. The caller needs the\n\u003e Push access right (a fast-forward `UPDATE`) on a writable, ordinary branch (not\n\u003e a tag, `refs/meta/*`, or a Gerrit-internal ref). Ref-update and commit\n\u003e validators run (including plugin validators), but there is no receive-pack, so\n\u003e push-only policies are not applied: signed-push / push-certificate verification\n\u003e is skipped and receive-pack object checks such as `receive.maxObjectSizeLimit`\n\u003e are not enforced.\n```\n\nQuestion (for a maintainer): signed-push policy. A branch can be configured with `requireSignedPush`, but that is enforced only on the receive path — `SignedPushPreReceiveHook` rejects a push whose `ReceivePack.getPushCertificate()` is null.\n\nThis endpoint has no push certificate (the caller is authenticated by their HTTP session, not a signed push), so it cannot verify or honor that setting — the same as every other server-side write path (change edits, create-change, cherry-pick, submit, `CreateBranch`), none of which go through receive-pack.\n\nMy recommendation: treat this endpoint as exempt from signed-push and document it, consistent with those paths. \"Simulating\" a signed push here isn\u0027t possible, since there is no certificate to check.\n\nThe only alternative would be to reject the write when the target branch has `requireSignedPush` set. I\u0027d only do that if you want this endpoint to be stricter than the rest of Gerrit\u0027s server-side APIs.",
